    introCopy: "The best interview prep starts with anticipating what you'll actually be asked, and a job posting is full of clues about that if you know how to read it. This tool analyzes a posting's listed responsibilities and requirements and generates a realistic mix of technical and behavioral questions you're likely to face, so you can prepare answers in advance rather than improvising under pressure. It's meant to work from the employer's side of the equation — the job posting — whereas resume-to-likely-interview-questions works from your own resume to predict what they'll ask about you specifically.",
